<!-- cell: title-page id="804f897f-4f36-44b4-a870-58cd65f7dc22" title="The Hands of St Aldric's" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="2f5b48d6-d3cc-441f-92e1-9f42bc1675ab" -->

On Monday morning the clock on the tower of St Aldric's said nothing at all. Its face was still there, pale and round above the village green, but both of its gilded hands were gone.

By ten o'clock Little Dunmore had decided it was the Pratt boys. By eleven it had decided it was the Pratt boys' cousins from Ely. Detective Inspector Ruth Abara, who had driven forty minutes for what the desk sergeant called a nice easy one, listened to all of it and wrote none of it down.

Reverend Hollis met her at the lych gate. He was a tall, gentle man with soil under his fingernails and a cardigan buttoned wrong. "A terrible thing," he said. "Those hands were three hundred years old."

"Who has a key to the tower?" asked Abara.

"Only me. And the bell-ringers, but they've no reason to climb to the clock."

The tower stair was narrow and dusty, every step furred grey. Every step except the last flight to the clock chamber, which had been swept so clean the stone shone. In the chamber the clock mechanism was undamaged. The bolts that had held the hands had been undone with care, by someone with the right spanner and plenty of time.

"Thieves don't sweep up after themselves," said Abara.

Back in the vestry she noticed the bucket. It stood beneath a brown stain in the ceiling, half full of rainwater, and beside it on the desk lay an invoice from a roofing firm in Cambridge, stamped PAID IN FULL. The date was Saturday.

"The diocese said the roof could wait until spring," said Hollis quietly. He did not look at the invoice. He looked at the bucket. "It couldn't. There's a family of five who sit under that stain every Sunday."

"Where did the money come from, Reverend?"

He sat down heavily. "A dealer in Norwich. He said gilt hands of that age were worth four thousand pounds to the right collector. I swept the stairs because I couldn't bear to leave a mess in God's house. Isn't that ridiculous? I could steal from it, but I couldn't leave it dirty."

Abara closed her notebook. Outside, a child was asking her mother why the clock had stopped.

"You'll tell the parish council yourself," said Abara. "Tonight. And the Pratt boys will get an apology from the pulpit."

A month later the clock on St Aldric's had new hands, carved from oak and painted gold by the bell-ringers. They were slightly crooked, and they kept perfect time.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="89170406-bed0-4325-9494-39a9b06299f5" title="Detective Inspector Ruth Abara" words="60-89, 209-216, 359-362" -->

The detective sent to a 'nice easy one', who ignores the village gossip and reads the case from a swept staircase and a bucket under a leak.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="aad8258c-1d04-4289-bde6-7dda0e7983fc" title="Reverend Hollis" words="98-113, 273-284, 348-358" -->

The gentle vicar who stole the church clock's gilded hands to pay for a roof the diocese would not mend, and who swept up after himself.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="99c1eb95-7422-44a1-95ae-36ffa469d2ef" title="St Aldric's church, Little Dunmore" words="15-34, 149-159, 225-256" -->

The village church, its silent clock above the green, its dusty tower stair and its leaking vestry roof.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="41673c0f-b4d6-488f-aa0a-623281362cc6" title="Theft" words="0-14, 187-208, 310-329" -->

The theft of the clock's three-hundred-year-old gilded hands, carefully unbolted and sold to a dealer in Norwich.

<!-- cell: plot id="1f8f3e4f-fa81-4d37-9aed-fe7817f83306" title="The stolen hands" words="0-34, 35-46, 160-178, 209-216, 217-260, 299-305, 306-344, 375-394, 395-414" -->

The clock's hands vanish and the village blames the Pratt boys; Abara reads the swept stair and the paid roofing invoice, the vicar confesses, and the clock gets new oak hands.
